WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.425 --> 00:00:06.681
Hi, welcome back to
the Nagarjuna series.

00:00:06.681 --> 00:00:09.824
And we're now in class number three.

00:00:09.824 --> 00:00:11.808
We're ready to attack the chapters.

00:00:11.808 --> 00:00:14.764
We're ready to get into the six chapters

00:00:14.764 --> 00:00:17.025
of the 27 chapters,

00:00:17.025 --> 00:00:20.895
which we're going to cover
in this particular session

00:00:20.895 --> 00:00:23.895
of Nagarjuna teaching, session four.

00:00:25.433 --> 00:00:29.116
This first chapter we're
going to cover is Chapter 18.

00:00:29.116 --> 00:00:32.938
And as I mentioned earlier,
we're going to do six chapters,

00:00:32.938 --> 00:00:36.846
18, 19, 20, 21, 22, 23.

00:00:36.846 --> 00:00:41.780
And pay close attention to Chapter 18.

00:00:42.746 --> 00:00:46.274
I'm going to give you the
names of these six chapters

00:00:46.274 --> 00:00:48.441
just to wet your appetite.

00:00:49.524 --> 00:00:51.342
And then we'll go into them.

00:00:51.342 --> 00:00:56.009
By the way, I hope as I mention
these six chapter names,

00:00:56.970 --> 00:01:00.387
that you are already
in the mood to guess...

00:01:02.930 --> 00:01:04.763
what's the connection?

00:01:07.301 --> 00:01:09.878
And if you're like me, you're
going to hear the six names,

00:01:09.878 --> 00:01:13.805
and you're going to say
"I don't see exactly

00:01:13.805 --> 00:01:16.183
how one would flow into the other."

00:01:16.183 --> 00:01:18.446
But that's not my problem.
That's your problem.

00:01:18.446 --> 00:01:20.217
Okay, here we go.

00:01:20.217 --> 00:01:22.967
Chapter 18 is called,

00:01:22.967 --> 00:01:26.793
"An Investigation of
the Person and Things."

00:01:26.793 --> 00:01:30.564
"An Investigation of
the Person and Things."

00:01:30.564 --> 00:01:33.243
And most of you know this classic division

00:01:33.243 --> 00:01:36.416
of all emptiness, into the emptiness

00:01:36.416 --> 00:01:40.225
of Michael and the emptiness of things.

00:01:40.225 --> 00:01:44.259
Or sometimes you can
say the emptiness of me

00:01:44.259 --> 00:01:48.210
and the emptiness of mine,
meaning all the things around me

00:01:48.210 --> 00:01:49.914
and the other parts of me.

00:01:49.914 --> 00:01:53.891
So you can say, now we're going
to investigate the emptiness

00:01:53.891 --> 00:01:57.316
of the person, and then
sometimes you can say things

00:01:57.316 --> 00:01:59.056
in the universe.

00:01:59.056 --> 00:02:03.334
Sometimes they specifically
say the parts of Michael.

00:02:03.334 --> 00:02:07.342
And some sophisticated
commentators will say,

00:02:07.342 --> 00:02:10.215
the universe as one of
the parts of Michael,

00:02:10.215 --> 00:02:12.701
because Michael perceives the universe.

00:02:12.701 --> 00:02:17.701
So really the universe becomes
just another part of Michael.

00:02:18.466 --> 00:02:20.042
And I wanted to point out

00:02:20.042 --> 00:02:24.459
that this chapter we're going
to start with, Chapter 18,

00:02:25.370 --> 00:02:28.489
it's a really, really popular chapter.

00:02:28.489 --> 00:02:33.053
I would say, as I mentioned
before, Chapter 18

00:02:33.053 --> 00:02:34.311
and Chapter 24,

00:02:34.311 --> 00:02:36.735
which we're going to cover next session,

00:02:36.735 --> 00:02:38.368
of Nagarjuna teachings.

00:02:38.368 --> 00:02:40.849
18 is extremely popular.

00:02:40.849 --> 00:02:43.104
You've heard me talk about it.

00:02:43.104 --> 00:02:47.834
You've heard many teachers
talk about this emptiness of me

00:02:47.834 --> 00:02:49.361
and the emptiness of my parts.

00:02:49.361 --> 00:02:53.278
And it's an extremely
important emptiness. Why?

00:02:54.132 --> 00:02:56.892
Because it's the first link,

00:02:56.892 --> 00:03:01.093
misunderstanding me and
misunderstanding my parts,

00:03:01.093 --> 00:03:03.214
is the first link of the Wheel of Life.

00:03:03.214 --> 00:03:04.047
It's called...

00:03:04.047 --> 00:03:05.306
What's the technical name?

00:03:05.306 --> 00:03:08.158
The strange technical name of that?

00:03:08.158 --> 00:03:10.952
'JIG TSOGS LA LTA BA

00:03:10.952 --> 00:03:14.462
The view of the perishable assemblage.

00:03:14.462 --> 00:03:17.912
And it's a special kind of
misunderstanding of the world

00:03:17.912 --> 00:03:19.910
which triggers all our personal suffering.

00:03:19.910 --> 00:03:22.743
So it's one that you should learn.

00:03:23.589 --> 00:03:28.393
All right, so, I actually had
some Mixed Nuts help me out,

00:03:28.393 --> 00:03:31.810
especially Word Smith and Nick Lashaw.

00:03:31.810 --> 00:03:36.810
And I said check all
the chapters of the 27,

00:03:36.927 --> 00:03:40.761
tell me which are the most quoted chapters

00:03:40.761 --> 00:03:42.271
in the last 1,000 years

00:03:42.271 --> 00:03:45.576
of commentary by the
great Tibetan writers.

00:03:45.576 --> 00:03:49.088
And they said, "Yeah, this Chapter 18

00:03:49.088 --> 00:03:51.820
"is a very, very popular chapter."

00:03:51.820 --> 00:03:55.466
And the only one which is
close to it is Chapter 24.

00:03:55.466 --> 00:04:00.466
24 is an examination of
the four higher truths,

00:04:00.758 --> 00:04:03.191
the four Arya truths.

00:04:03.191 --> 00:04:07.795
That's going to be actually
the single most popular chapter

00:04:07.795 --> 00:04:08.690
of all of them.

00:04:08.690 --> 00:04:11.112
So we'll get to that next term.

00:04:11.112 --> 00:04:15.636
But in this term, we're
starting with Chapter 18,

00:04:15.636 --> 00:04:20.636
which by itself is then probably
the most important chapter

00:04:21.064 --> 00:04:22.655
of the whole work.

00:04:22.655 --> 00:04:24.588
So get yourself ready.

00:04:24.588 --> 00:04:27.461
Get your thinking cap on for Chapter 18.

00:04:27.461 --> 00:04:31.703
Chapter 19, is "An Investigation of Time."

00:04:31.703 --> 00:04:33.767
Past, present and future.

00:04:33.767 --> 00:04:36.922
How does that flow from
the emptiness of me

00:04:36.922 --> 00:04:38.673
and my universe

00:04:38.673 --> 00:04:40.673
is an Einstein question.

00:04:41.547 --> 00:04:44.748
So you guys are supposed
to think about that.

00:04:44.748 --> 00:04:49.089
From the investigating
the emptiness of Michael

00:04:49.089 --> 00:04:52.590
and Michael's world, Michael's universe,

00:04:52.590 --> 00:04:57.062
we go directly to investigating
the nature of time itself.

00:04:57.062 --> 00:05:02.062
And we included a lot of those
ideas from the 19th chapter,

00:05:02.652 --> 00:05:06.319
in the DCI presentation of time management

00:05:08.317 --> 00:05:12.466
because there are ways to modify time.

00:05:12.466 --> 00:05:15.401
There are ways to stretch time.

00:05:15.401 --> 00:05:17.678
If you want to get more things done,

00:05:17.678 --> 00:05:19.879
I encourage you to take that course,

00:05:19.879 --> 00:05:21.931
the time management course of DCI.

00:05:21.931 --> 00:05:25.151
I think it's Level 11.

00:05:25.151 --> 00:05:26.308
Okay, good.

00:05:26.308 --> 00:05:31.057
Okay, Chapter 20 is a very sexy chapter.

00:05:31.057 --> 00:05:34.000
My teacher would say, "Oh? The
other chapters aren't sexy?"

00:05:34.000 --> 00:05:37.753
No, they are, but this one's really sexy.

00:05:37.753 --> 00:05:39.811
It's called, "An Investigation

00:05:39.811 --> 00:05:43.970
of How Causes and
Conditions Come Together,"

00:05:43.970 --> 00:05:45.883
how they converge.

00:05:45.883 --> 00:05:50.883
And there's an expression there
called, what's is it called?

00:05:51.393 --> 00:05:54.043
The ultimate convergence.

00:05:55.067 --> 00:05:59.352
Consummate convergence
of causes and conditions.

00:05:59.352 --> 00:06:02.047
What's it mean for causes and conditions

00:06:02.047 --> 00:06:04.692
to come together in a perfect way?

00:06:04.692 --> 00:06:07.417
And what are the implications

00:06:07.417 --> 00:06:12.349
of the perfect coming together
of causes and conditions?

00:06:12.349 --> 00:06:15.632
Shouldn't that trigger
a result immediately?

00:06:15.632 --> 00:06:18.084
Once you get all the necessary causes

00:06:18.084 --> 00:06:19.383
and all the necessary conditions:

00:06:19.383 --> 00:06:22.933
you have a flower seed,
then you have fertilizer,

00:06:22.933 --> 00:06:26.633
warmth, water, time, gardener,

00:06:26.633 --> 00:06:28.704
then shouldn't that consummate collection

00:06:28.704 --> 00:06:32.878
of causes and conditions
immediately trigger a result?

00:06:32.878 --> 00:06:35.128
And where's the emptiness there?

00:06:35.128 --> 00:06:36.436
That's Chapter 20.

00:06:36.436 --> 00:06:41.436
Chapter 21. Once you talk about

00:06:41.439 --> 00:06:44.464
and all we've talked about so
far in the first 20 chapters

00:06:44.464 --> 00:06:46.989
would be how things happen.

00:06:46.989 --> 00:06:48.979
Then wouldn't it be natural to talk about

00:06:48.979 --> 00:06:53.963
how things die. How do things
die? How do things pass away?

00:06:55.072 --> 00:06:57.627
Whoops, I just gave you a connection!

00:06:57.627 --> 00:06:59.794
But, that's up to you to...

00:07:00.796 --> 00:07:01.629
What do we call it?

00:07:01.629 --> 00:07:02.796
...to explore it.

00:07:04.226 --> 00:07:07.069
How do things occur and then pass away?

00:07:07.069 --> 00:07:08.561
Chapter 21.

00:07:08.561 --> 00:07:12.593
Chapter 22. Why it jumps in here?

00:07:12.593 --> 00:07:15.547
"An Investigation of Buddhas."

00:07:15.547 --> 00:07:20.213
Wow, that's cool. You
didn't talk about Buddhas

00:07:20.213 --> 00:07:22.606
pretty much in this whole book,

00:07:22.606 --> 00:07:25.673
and then suddenly, after
investigating time, etc.

00:07:25.673 --> 00:07:29.756
you jump off, Mr. A.D.D. Nagarjuna,

00:07:31.043 --> 00:07:34.662
you jump off into the
emptiness of Buddhas.

00:07:34.662 --> 00:07:35.623
What's that all about?

00:07:35.623 --> 00:07:37.134
Where's the connection?

00:07:37.134 --> 00:07:41.063
We're going to explore that in Chapter 22.

00:07:41.063 --> 00:07:43.563
Chapter 23, really interesting

00:07:44.994 --> 00:07:47.947
to explain how this flows
from the last chapter

00:07:47.947 --> 00:07:50.947
because Chapter 23

00:07:50.947 --> 00:07:54.710
is dedicated to examining the mistake

00:07:54.710 --> 00:07:58.030
we make in our life, the
mistakes we make in our life.

00:07:58.030 --> 00:08:01.972
Chapter 23 is pretty much
dedicated to mistakes,

00:08:01.972 --> 00:08:05.384
especially in perceiving things,

00:08:05.384 --> 00:08:07.542
not understanding the emptiness of things.

00:08:07.542 --> 00:08:09.325
So again,

00:08:09.325 --> 00:08:13.317
it's up to you guys to explore the flow.

00:08:13.317 --> 00:08:15.928
I'll give you the six chapters again.

00:08:15.928 --> 00:08:20.928
18: persons and things,
the emptiness of Michael

00:08:21.279 --> 00:08:22.759
and his universe.

00:08:22.759 --> 00:08:26.141
19: we're going to investigate time.

00:08:26.141 --> 00:08:30.224
20: when causes and
conditions come all together,

00:08:31.098 --> 00:08:35.016
into what I like to call
a consummate convergence.

00:08:35.016 --> 00:08:37.477
Perfect convergence of
causes and conditions.

00:08:37.477 --> 00:08:40.886
How do we look into the emptiness of that?

00:08:40.886 --> 00:08:44.469
How could that not have
potency inherent within it

00:08:44.469 --> 00:08:47.269
when all the causes and
conditions are assembled?

00:08:47.269 --> 00:08:50.743
How could that not have
some inherent power?

00:08:50.743 --> 00:08:53.674
Because things always
pop up right after that.

00:08:53.674 --> 00:08:58.091
Chapter 22: we'll look into
Those Gone Thus, Tathagata.

00:09:03.128 --> 00:09:05.634
21: is how things occur
and then pass away.

00:09:05.634 --> 00:09:08.807
22: is the one with
Those Gone Thus, sorry.

00:09:08.807 --> 00:09:12.237
And then 23 is an investigation of error.

00:09:12.237 --> 00:09:16.904
All right, like in the chapter
numbers, all right, good.

00:09:18.216 --> 00:09:20.049
All right, here we go.

00:09:22.316 --> 00:09:27.316
Each time we do a class
together, now starting today,

00:09:27.319 --> 00:09:32.171
in this class, I would like to teach you

00:09:32.171 --> 00:09:35.159
three important Sanskrit words.

00:09:35.159 --> 00:09:38.613
They say that all Buddhas speak Sanskrit

00:09:38.613 --> 00:09:40.592
after they've become enlightened,

00:09:40.592 --> 00:09:45.259
and they say that the syllables
of the Sanskrit language

00:09:47.643 --> 00:09:49.009
have a deep,

00:09:51.651 --> 00:09:55.885
almost inexplicable
effect on your chakras,

00:09:55.885 --> 00:09:57.343
on your mind, on your body.

00:09:57.343 --> 00:10:01.725
Somehow Sanskrit language
has an immediate effect

00:10:01.725 --> 00:10:03.834
on your body and mind.

00:10:03.834 --> 00:10:06.215
So I thought we could talk a little bit

00:10:06.215 --> 00:10:10.888
about the important words in
each chapter that we cover.

00:10:10.888 --> 00:10:12.699
And if you were going
to read the Sanskrit,

00:10:12.699 --> 00:10:15.681
we're very fortunate that the Sanskrit

00:10:15.681 --> 00:10:18.938
for this "Wisdom" book still exists.

00:10:18.938 --> 00:10:21.298
We can still read the Sanskrit.

00:10:21.298 --> 00:10:24.358
It's a wonderful place
to memorize Sanskrit,

00:10:24.358 --> 00:10:27.597
especially the opening two verses.

00:10:27.597 --> 00:10:30.963
It's a wonderful place if
you want to learn Sanskrit,

00:10:30.963 --> 00:10:34.179
to learn it in the way that
Nagarjuna used Sanskrit,

00:10:34.179 --> 00:10:39.179
which is probably one of the
greatest authors in Sanskrit.

00:10:39.244 --> 00:10:44.038
So we're going to explore,
going to have an investigation

00:10:44.038 --> 00:10:48.006
of three Sanskrit words during each class,

00:10:48.006 --> 00:10:51.931
and these are words that are
important for that chapter.

00:10:51.931 --> 00:10:54.421
Here's the first one today.

00:10:54.421 --> 00:10:57.387
I want to talk about the word 'aātman'.

00:10:57.387 --> 00:10:58.789
Say ātman.

00:10:58.789 --> 00:10:59.924
- [All] ātman

00:10:59.924 --> 00:11:03.979
- Yeah, ātman means BDAG in Tibetan,

00:11:03.979 --> 00:11:07.445
which means a self or a self nature.

00:11:07.445 --> 00:11:11.715
You probably heard the
word Mahātmā Ghandhi

00:11:11.715 --> 00:11:14.006
which means the great being.

00:11:14.006 --> 00:11:19.006
'The great self', meaning Ghandhi himself,

00:11:19.592 --> 00:11:21.792
Mohandas K. Ghandhi.

00:11:23.442 --> 00:11:26.359
So ātman means self or self nature.

00:11:28.228 --> 00:11:32.719
I like the parts of this
word. I think it's very sexy.

00:11:32.719 --> 00:11:37.388
The 'ā' the initial long
'ā' used to be a short 'a'.

00:11:37.388 --> 00:11:40.669
And sometimes a short 'a'
will change to a long 'ā'

00:11:40.669 --> 00:11:42.970
at the beginning of a Sanskrit word.

00:11:42.970 --> 00:11:45.587
So it used to be atman.

00:11:45.587 --> 00:11:48.920
And this initial 'a' in a Sanskrit word,

00:11:50.127 --> 00:11:54.719
is called the privitive 'a' in
Greek, it survives in Greek.

00:11:54.719 --> 00:11:56.769
And it means 'not'.

00:11:56.769 --> 00:12:01.174
So when an 'a' comes at the
beginning of a word in Sanskrit,

00:12:01.174 --> 00:12:03.644
it can mean 'not'.

00:12:03.644 --> 00:12:08.227
And that survives in English
in words like apolitical,

00:12:09.092 --> 00:12:13.633
or atheist, or apathetic,

00:12:13.633 --> 00:12:15.596
meaning don't care.

00:12:15.596 --> 00:12:20.596
And you see an initial 'a' meaning 'not'.

00:12:21.374 --> 00:12:25.541
The root to Sanskrit 'tam', T-M or, T-A-M,

00:12:26.614 --> 00:12:29.689
means to be separated.

00:12:30.808 --> 00:12:35.391
And so ātman, the word for
self, and the word for atom,

00:12:37.723 --> 00:12:42.140
means inseparable, a coherent,
single, unitary, soul.

00:12:46.141 --> 00:12:48.307
Soul or self of a thing.

00:12:48.307 --> 00:12:52.640
An atom means indivisible
particle, cannot be split.

00:12:55.087 --> 00:12:57.883
Once you split an atom
into smaller particles,

00:12:57.883 --> 00:13:01.018
it loses its elemental identity,

00:13:01.018 --> 00:13:04.601
and it no longer has
unique characteristics

00:13:05.840 --> 00:13:07.673
that other atoms don't have.

00:13:07.673 --> 00:13:10.340
So atom means that unsplittable,

00:13:11.231 --> 00:13:14.777
ātman means a soul,

00:13:14.777 --> 00:13:18.855
and when you put ātman in a sentence,

00:13:18.855 --> 00:13:22.522
the 'n' drops off, when
you use it as a soul

00:13:23.463 --> 00:13:25.129
then it's 'ātma'.

00:13:25.129 --> 00:13:28.301
Another example of that is 'yogin',

00:13:28.301 --> 00:13:30.217
which means 'a practitioner of yoga',

00:13:30.217 --> 00:13:33.550
and then, when you use it in a sentence,

00:13:34.691 --> 00:13:36.651
what's it called?

00:13:36.651 --> 00:13:39.838
as the noun of a sentence,
it loses the 'n',

00:13:39.838 --> 00:13:44.838
it becomes 'yogi', a
practitioner of yoga is a yogi.

00:13:45.588 --> 00:13:48.773
But the original form is yogin.

00:13:48.773 --> 00:13:53.757
So you might see ātman as
ātma, like in Mahātmā Ghandhi.

00:13:56.282 --> 00:14:01.282
And the classical example
of the use of the root tam,

00:14:01.880 --> 00:14:04.963
is in this picture that you see here.

00:14:05.818 --> 00:14:09.378
This is a classic stone church,

00:14:09.378 --> 00:14:11.599
stone temple in Eastern Europe.

00:14:11.599 --> 00:14:14.541
And typically they had a wall,

00:14:14.541 --> 00:14:18.556
a stone wall would separate the village

00:14:18.556 --> 00:14:21.473
from the church or temple.

00:14:21.473 --> 00:14:26.448
And then those structures
became called 'temple'.

00:14:28.881 --> 00:14:30.426
'The place which is set apart',

00:14:30.426 --> 00:14:33.599
so that's a use of the root tam.

00:14:33.599 --> 00:14:36.891
A temple is built on that root also.

00:14:36.891 --> 00:14:40.843
So atom or ātma means a real self,

00:14:40.843 --> 00:14:43.329
a solid core to all things.

00:14:43.329 --> 00:14:46.329
Once you say it that way,

00:14:47.371 --> 00:14:51.278
does Michael and does Michael's universe

00:14:51.278 --> 00:14:52.778
have a solid core,

00:14:54.245 --> 00:14:57.000
that's independent of my perceptions,

00:14:57.000 --> 00:15:00.804
or independent of my
actions towards others,

00:15:00.804 --> 00:15:04.395
independent of my kindness degree,

00:15:04.395 --> 00:15:08.543
the amount of my kindness,
that would be ātman?

00:15:08.543 --> 00:15:13.543
That would be a solid core to Michael,

00:15:13.687 --> 00:15:17.710
which is independent of how
Michael treats other people.

00:15:17.710 --> 00:15:20.793
If the world doesn't come from karma,

00:15:22.920 --> 00:15:25.427
if the quality of my
life is not determined

00:15:25.427 --> 00:15:27.610
by how well I treat others,

00:15:27.610 --> 00:15:30.636
then atom, then I'm an ātman.

00:15:30.636 --> 00:15:33.999
Then I'm a self-existing
self, and I'm not affected

00:15:33.999 --> 00:15:36.402
by how I treat others.

00:15:36.402 --> 00:15:38.965
So that's one word I'd like
you to learn in the Sanskrit.

00:15:38.965 --> 00:15:41.510
Now, I promised you three.

00:15:41.510 --> 00:15:44.177
The next one is skandha,

00:15:45.367 --> 00:15:49.450
which means in Tibetan PHUNG PO.

00:15:51.890 --> 00:15:52.723
In English,

00:15:52.723 --> 00:15:57.223
when we describe a person,
we say 'the body and mind'.

00:15:57.223 --> 00:16:01.190
In Buddhism, we say 'the five parts',

00:16:01.190 --> 00:16:03.797
the five parts of the person,

00:16:03.797 --> 00:16:07.399
and each of those parts
in classical Buddhism

00:16:07.399 --> 00:16:10.608
is not called part. It's called a heap,

00:16:10.608 --> 00:16:14.966
or a pile, and the Abhidharma says,

00:16:14.966 --> 00:16:16.974
,SPUNGS DANG SKYE SGO RIGS KYI DON,

00:16:16.974 --> 00:16:18.949
,PHUNG PO SKYE MCHED KHAMS RNAMS YIN,

00:16:18.949 --> 00:16:20.432
The reason, why do we call...

00:16:22.317 --> 00:16:24.428
Why don't we just say body and mind?

00:16:24.428 --> 00:16:27.607
Why do we say the five piles of a person?

00:16:27.607 --> 00:16:31.898
It's that each part of a
person, in western terminology,

00:16:31.898 --> 00:16:36.898
the mind and the body, they
also have multiple components.

00:16:36.962 --> 00:16:40.544
So we don't say body and mind,

00:16:40.544 --> 00:16:44.127
we say a pile of multiple
component groups.

00:16:45.603 --> 00:16:48.610
Where the word skandha comes from,

00:16:48.610 --> 00:16:51.091
if you trace Sanskrit back to older roots,

00:16:51.091 --> 00:16:53.617
which are called Indo-European roots,

00:16:53.617 --> 00:16:55.972
there's a root, skend-,

00:16:55.972 --> 00:17:00.955
and that's the root in English
for scatter and shatter.

00:17:01.933 --> 00:17:06.100
So you've got this idea
of a scattering of leaves,

00:17:06.963 --> 00:17:10.929
meaning each part of my
body, sorry, my whole body,

00:17:10.929 --> 00:17:14.866
and my whole mind, is made
up of multiple, smaller parts

00:17:14.866 --> 00:17:16.272
and smaller components.

00:17:16.272 --> 00:17:20.821
In that sense, my body and
my mind are both piles.

00:17:20.821 --> 00:17:24.763
In classical Buddhism, we
don't say body and mind.

00:17:24.763 --> 00:17:29.070
We say the five components
of body and mind.

00:17:29.070 --> 00:17:33.233
Mind has four components
and body has one component.

00:17:33.233 --> 00:17:36.594
So we have a physical
side of us, one component,

00:17:36.594 --> 00:17:39.795
composed of many parts, scatterings.

00:17:39.795 --> 00:17:42.413
And then we have the
four mental components,

00:17:42.413 --> 00:17:46.088
which is your general awareness;

00:17:46.958 --> 00:17:51.172
your tendency to feel at
all times, night and day;

00:17:51.172 --> 00:17:53.976
your tendency to discriminate,
this is good, this is bad,

00:17:53.976 --> 00:17:57.425
this is tall, this is short,
this is blue, this is green;

00:17:57.425 --> 00:18:00.675
and then the other parts of your mind

00:18:00.675 --> 00:18:02.689
are classically about 50 other parts

00:18:02.689 --> 00:18:04.564
or components of your mind.

00:18:05.664 --> 00:18:07.114
They are divided out

00:18:07.114 --> 00:18:11.722
in Buddhism to five skandha,
five piles, five heaps

00:18:11.722 --> 00:18:16.463
of things which you
consist of. It's important

00:18:16.463 --> 00:18:20.315
in the 18th chapter, which is
a discussion of the emptiness

00:18:20.315 --> 00:18:22.224
of the person and their parts,

00:18:22.224 --> 00:18:24.111
because this is the most typical division

00:18:24.111 --> 00:18:26.524
of the parts to a person.

00:18:26.524 --> 00:18:28.805
It's the five parts.

00:18:28.805 --> 00:18:31.422
In the west, we say body and mind.

00:18:31.422 --> 00:18:34.714
When I say five heaps, five skandhas,

00:18:34.714 --> 00:18:39.108
five parts of a person, I want
you to think body and mind.

00:18:39.108 --> 00:18:41.080
That's like a perfect translation

00:18:41.080 --> 00:18:45.414
into western thinking: body and mind.

00:18:45.414 --> 00:18:49.291
Does your body have a core,
does your mind have a core?

00:18:49.291 --> 00:18:53.208
Do body and mind together,
as Tim, have a core,

00:18:55.414 --> 00:18:58.016
an unchanging core, an ātman?

00:18:58.016 --> 00:18:59.667
Did I promise you three words?

00:18:59.667 --> 00:19:03.167
Did I have a third one here, no, just two.

00:19:04.776 --> 00:19:07.109
Sorry about that.

00:19:09.577 --> 00:19:13.278
Now I'm going to go back to
the beginning of the chapter.

00:19:13.278 --> 00:19:15.945
He says, "when you talk about...

00:19:18.426 --> 00:19:20.645
"When you start your
examination of emptiness,"

00:19:20.645 --> 00:19:23.807
which is what he's doing
with the 18th chapter.

00:19:23.807 --> 00:19:27.474
Now he's specifically starting our journey

00:19:27.474 --> 00:19:30.230
into emptiness itself.

00:19:30.230 --> 00:19:32.814
He says, "Let's proceed in five steps."

00:19:32.814 --> 00:19:35.026
Let's proceed in five steps.

00:19:35.026 --> 00:19:38.505
Generally, I'm not
reading you the commentary

00:19:38.505 --> 00:19:41.301
because it would take a few years.

00:19:41.301 --> 00:19:43.834
But when I see a part of the commentary

00:19:43.834 --> 00:19:47.326
which is particularly sexy or interesting,

00:19:47.326 --> 00:19:49.718
or will help you understand emptiness,

00:19:49.718 --> 00:19:51.602
then I'll pause and
I'll just read straight

00:19:51.602 --> 00:19:54.338
from the commentary, so that's
what I'm going to do now.

00:19:54.338 --> 00:19:56.054
DANG PO LA LNGA,

00:19:56.054 --> 00:20:01.054
When we examine, how do
you get into the idea

00:20:01.093 --> 00:20:02.937
that things are not themselves?

00:20:02.937 --> 00:20:07.937
How do you first enter?
How do you first take off

00:20:08.018 --> 00:20:10.957
into this idea that a
thing is not themselves?

00:20:10.957 --> 00:20:13.981
And he says, "Five steps."

00:20:13.981 --> 00:20:18.651
If you want to explore how
things are not themselves,

00:20:18.651 --> 00:20:23.038
let's look at it from five
different points of view.

00:20:23.038 --> 00:20:24.305
Number one.

00:20:24.305 --> 00:20:26.210
DE KHO NA NYID LA 'JUG TSUL,

00:20:26.210 --> 00:20:28.735
I'm going to describe to you

00:20:28.735 --> 00:20:31.342
how you first get into emptiness.

00:20:31.342 --> 00:20:34.854
He calls it here suchness,
same thing, tatva.

00:20:34.854 --> 00:20:39.183
I'm going to explain to you
how you enter into suchness.

00:20:39.183 --> 00:20:41.306
First thing. Second:

00:20:41.306 --> 00:20:42.531
LUNG DANG 'GAL SPONG,

00:20:42.531 --> 00:20:44.297
LUNG DANG 'GAL SPONG,

00:20:44.297 --> 00:20:47.598
Look, I know Buddha talked
about the parts of the person.

00:20:47.598 --> 00:20:50.098
I know the Buddha talked about

00:20:51.190 --> 00:20:55.332
apparently solid powers of a person.

00:20:55.332 --> 00:20:59.749
But look, I'm not contradicting
what he said about people,

00:20:59.749 --> 00:21:01.524
in the first turning of the Wheel.

00:21:01.524 --> 00:21:03.257
I'm not contradicting that.

00:21:04.221 --> 00:21:08.554
What we say when we say
Michael and Michael's parts,

00:21:09.637 --> 00:21:13.176
are not coming from Michael
and Michael's parts,

00:21:13.176 --> 00:21:18.176
but what we mean by that is clear.

00:21:18.552 --> 00:21:22.285
If we can show how we
are not contradicting

00:21:22.285 --> 00:21:23.519
what the Buddha said

00:21:23.519 --> 00:21:26.673
when he first started to
teach. He talked a lot

00:21:26.673 --> 00:21:29.982
about things as if they were solid,

00:21:29.982 --> 00:21:32.109
and if they had their own identity,

00:21:32.109 --> 00:21:35.127
as if they had their own identity.

00:21:35.127 --> 00:21:38.307
And basically, we're just going to show

00:21:38.307 --> 00:21:42.410
that in the beginning
of his teaching career,

00:21:42.410 --> 00:21:45.351
Lord Buddha talked about things

00:21:45.351 --> 00:21:50.140
as sort of concrete things,
because he was warming up

00:21:50.140 --> 00:21:53.175
to get to tell you why
they're not concrete.

00:21:53.175 --> 00:21:55.187
He was just warming up.

00:21:55.187 --> 00:21:57.821
LUNG DANG 'GAL SPONG

00:21:57.821 --> 00:22:01.336
We will work out these
seeming contradictions

00:22:01.336 --> 00:22:03.936
between the later teachings on emptiness

00:22:03.936 --> 00:22:06.845
and the early teachings
on the parts of a person,

00:22:06.845 --> 00:22:10.389
in which they seem to be so concrete.

00:22:10.389 --> 00:22:14.769
Third topic here, if you really
want to get into emptiness.

00:22:14.769 --> 00:22:18.101
DE KHO NA NYID LA BKRI BA'I RIM PA,

00:22:18.101 --> 00:22:21.050
What are the proper stages

00:22:21.050 --> 00:22:25.152
for introducing these
deep ideas to a student?

00:22:25.152 --> 00:22:26.569
You can't just sit down and say,

00:22:26.569 --> 00:22:30.729
"Hey, you're emptiness. You're
just made of emptiness."

00:22:30.729 --> 00:22:34.279
We should rather have a strategy.

00:22:35.275 --> 00:22:39.057
What's an intelligent, effective way

00:22:39.057 --> 00:22:41.462
to introduce emptiness to another person?

00:22:41.462 --> 00:22:43.005
If you have tried to talk

00:22:43.005 --> 00:22:46.988
about emptiness with your
family members, for example,

00:22:46.988 --> 00:22:50.679
you have immediately
encountered this problem.

00:22:50.679 --> 00:22:54.596
There are skillful ways
to start to talk about

00:22:54.596 --> 00:22:58.471
the meaning of emptiness,
for example, in the kitchen.

00:22:58.471 --> 00:23:00.779
And there are ways that
you shouldn't start talking

00:23:00.779 --> 00:23:04.152
about emptiness, which
will just confuse people.

00:23:04.152 --> 00:23:08.235
So the third step here is,
what are skillful ways,

00:23:09.420 --> 00:23:11.965
what are skillful strategies

00:23:11.965 --> 00:23:14.809
for introducing people to emptiness?

00:23:14.809 --> 00:23:16.377
I love the next section, which is,

00:23:16.377 --> 00:23:20.431
DE KHO NA NYID KYI MTSAN NYID,

00:23:20.431 --> 00:23:22.509
If emptiness itself is empty,

00:23:22.509 --> 00:23:26.221
does it have any defining characteristics?

00:23:26.221 --> 00:23:28.602
Does it have any special features

00:23:28.602 --> 00:23:30.270
which are unique to emptiness?

00:23:30.270 --> 00:23:31.519
And it does.

00:23:31.519 --> 00:23:34.186
And they are really
sexy. I really like them.

00:23:34.186 --> 00:23:38.571
So he's going to talk about
the defining characteristics,

00:23:38.571 --> 00:23:41.831
the special features of emptiness itself.

00:23:41.831 --> 00:23:46.550
We tend to think that if
emptiness means nothing,

00:23:46.550 --> 00:23:49.269
it shouldn't have special characteristics,

00:23:49.269 --> 00:23:54.269
or typical features, but
it's quite the opposite.

00:23:54.739 --> 00:23:59.739
Emptiness has many
features or characteristics

00:24:00.016 --> 00:24:01.627
which are typical of it,

00:24:01.627 --> 00:24:04.801
and Nagarjuna's explanation
of those characteristics

00:24:04.801 --> 00:24:08.362
is really, it's intriguing,
very intriguing.

00:24:08.362 --> 00:24:09.653
Okay, fifth.

00:24:09.653 --> 00:24:14.385
DON DE NGES PAR BSGRUB
DGOS PAR BSTAN PA'O,,

00:24:14.385 --> 00:24:17.885
And isn't it fitting that the last section

00:24:19.683 --> 00:24:22.488
in our introduction to emptiness

00:24:22.488 --> 00:24:27.488
should be how are you
going to improve your life

00:24:27.531 --> 00:24:29.554
by understanding emptiness?

00:24:29.554 --> 00:24:31.204
DON DE NGES PAR BSGRUB

00:24:31.204 --> 00:24:33.727
How are you going to put this emptiness

00:24:33.727 --> 00:24:37.540
into a practical application in your life

00:24:37.540 --> 00:24:40.174
to have a more successful
and meaningful life?

00:24:40.174 --> 00:24:43.983
So isn't that a wonderful final category

00:24:43.983 --> 00:24:46.637
that we'll be discussing?

00:24:46.637 --> 00:24:51.189
You learned this big idea,
this big theory of emptiness,

00:24:51.189 --> 00:24:53.754
but how is that going to improve your life

00:24:53.754 --> 00:24:56.020
and the lives of other people?

00:24:56.020 --> 00:24:59.603
So there we go. Here
we go, now, down to...

00:25:06.422 --> 00:25:08.922
I have a picture for you here

00:25:12.066 --> 00:25:15.127
of a lady on a swing, I guess.

00:25:15.127 --> 00:25:19.112
Yeah, people tend to think, I don't know,

00:25:19.112 --> 00:25:22.069
I saw this picture and I
thought, oh this is perfect.

00:25:22.069 --> 00:25:27.069
People kind of think that their
relationship to emptiness...

00:25:27.295 --> 00:25:30.566
You wouldn't say, "I have
a personal relationship

00:25:30.566 --> 00:25:31.702
with emptiness."

00:25:31.702 --> 00:25:33.703
It sounds kind of weird. It's like

00:25:33.703 --> 00:25:38.009
this lady's boyfriend
is the sun or something.

00:25:38.009 --> 00:25:42.179
And the fact is that there are ways

00:25:42.179 --> 00:25:45.584
in which you can have a
relationship to emptiness,

00:25:45.584 --> 00:25:49.737
almost a personal, intimate
relationship with emptiness.

00:25:49.737 --> 00:25:50.735
And that's a big part

00:25:50.735 --> 00:25:52.673
of the chapters we're going to cover.

00:25:52.673 --> 00:25:57.631
How can you start to have a meaningful,

00:25:58.871 --> 00:26:01.571
personal relationship with emptiness?

00:26:01.571 --> 00:26:03.904
We'll be going through that.

00:26:05.627 --> 00:26:10.627
And then he says, let's
start off on that first,

00:26:10.974 --> 00:26:14.369
if you remember the first
category, it was called,

00:26:14.369 --> 00:26:17.386
DE KHO NA NYID LA 'JUG TSUL

00:26:17.386 --> 00:26:18.953
How should we enter emptiness?

00:26:18.953 --> 00:26:22.310
Anybody want to tell me the
special implication of the word

00:26:22.310 --> 00:26:26.084
'JUG?

00:26:26.084 --> 00:26:27.392
It's the same one as starting off

00:26:27.392 --> 00:26:29.876
on a boat across the ocean.

00:26:29.876 --> 00:26:31.425
avatara

00:26:32.558 --> 00:26:37.508
So how should we get started

00:26:37.508 --> 00:26:39.156
on our journey to emptiness?

00:26:39.156 --> 00:26:41.465
What are the first steps
we're going to have to take?

00:26:41.465 --> 00:26:44.714
And then he says, "Oh, those first steps,

00:26:44.714 --> 00:26:47.406
"if they come themselves in two parts."

00:26:47.406 --> 00:26:50.264
The first steps you're
going to have to make

00:26:50.264 --> 00:26:54.206
to dig into emptiness
has two parts itself.

00:26:54.206 --> 00:26:58.098
And normally, I'm not reading
you his whole outline,

00:26:58.098 --> 00:27:00.515
which is really a long story

00:27:01.523 --> 00:27:04.504
how Choney Lama organizes Nagarjuna,

00:27:04.504 --> 00:27:08.523
which is extremely
beautiful, extremely helpful.

00:27:08.523 --> 00:27:10.846
But it would take us a year or two.

00:27:10.846 --> 00:27:13.970
And I am charting it. I'm
keeping a separate chart

00:27:13.970 --> 00:27:17.547
of the, what do you call
that thing, a mind map

00:27:17.547 --> 00:27:21.093
Of the outline, and it's beautiful.

00:27:21.093 --> 00:27:24.258
I'm keeping it separately,
but it's very complex.

00:27:24.258 --> 00:27:27.796
But I have some parts of his outline

00:27:27.796 --> 00:27:29.958
that are just too sexy to ignore.

00:27:29.958 --> 00:27:32.708
He says, "Entering emptiness,

00:27:34.326 --> 00:27:35.616
"like getting into a ship

00:27:35.616 --> 00:27:38.883
and starting across the Pacific ocean."

00:27:38.883 --> 00:27:40.300
It has two parts.

00:27:41.234 --> 00:27:43.084
LTA BA GTAN LA DBAB PA, and

00:27:43.084 --> 00:27:45.709
BSGOMS PAS NYES SKYON LDOG TSUL

00:27:46.809 --> 00:27:48.483
LTA BA GTAN LA DBAB PA and

00:27:48.483 --> 00:27:51.350
BSGOMS PAS NYES SKYON LDOG TSUL

00:27:51.350 --> 00:27:55.172
The exploration of
emptiness necessarily comes

00:27:55.172 --> 00:27:56.705
in two steps.

00:27:57.821 --> 00:28:00.738
A personal exploration of emptiness

00:28:01.604 --> 00:28:06.318
should involve two
separate steps. Number one:

00:28:06.318 --> 00:28:08.209
LTA BA GTAN LA DBAB PA

00:28:08.209 --> 00:28:12.618
You have to work hard
to learn the scriptures

00:28:12.618 --> 00:28:14.101
that describe emptiness.

00:28:14.101 --> 00:28:15.102
LTA BA GTAN LA DBAB PA

00:28:15.102 --> 00:28:18.810
means you got to work hard on...

00:28:18.810 --> 00:28:22.812
It's what we're doing now
is to struggle your way,

00:28:22.812 --> 00:28:25.711
fight your way through the
teachings on emptiness.

00:28:25.711 --> 00:28:30.208
You have to kind of submit and surrender.

00:28:30.208 --> 00:28:35.208
It's going to be a long
journey through a thick forest.

00:28:35.292 --> 00:28:39.126
Many scratchy trees and many muddy spots.

00:28:40.332 --> 00:28:45.332
But your first job is
to understand emptiness

00:28:45.632 --> 00:28:50.103
in a theoretical way, and
that implies the second step

00:28:50.103 --> 00:28:53.846
which is to put it into
practice in your life.

00:28:53.846 --> 00:28:57.428
Use it in your life. Use it
to become more successful.

00:28:57.428 --> 00:28:59.885
Use it to have a better family life.

00:28:59.885 --> 00:29:02.727
Use it to have a better business life.

00:29:02.727 --> 00:29:04.758
Use it to be a happier person

00:29:04.758 --> 00:29:06.799
throughout the course of your life.

00:29:06.799 --> 00:29:09.445
BSGOMS PAS NYES SKYON LDOG TSUL

00:29:09.445 --> 00:29:10.445
Second Step.

00:29:11.932 --> 00:29:16.346
Use those teachings on
emptiness, meditate on them,

00:29:16.346 --> 00:29:21.343
using the personalized instructions

00:29:21.343 --> 00:29:22.859
of John and Connie O'Brady.

00:29:25.331 --> 00:29:26.664
And by doing so,

00:29:27.876 --> 00:29:29.359
LDOG all your SKYONs

00:29:30.759 --> 00:29:32.375
Tim? SKYON MIN LDOG

00:29:32.375 --> 00:29:33.591
SKYON LDOG

00:29:36.641 --> 00:29:39.560
Yeah, stop all your problems in your life.

00:29:39.560 --> 00:29:41.483
SKYON means problems in your life.

00:29:41.483 --> 00:29:42.916
NYES PA also means

00:29:42.916 --> 00:29:43.749
problems in your life.

00:29:43.749 --> 00:29:44.707
NYES SKYON

00:29:44.707 --> 00:29:46.473
means problem problem.

00:29:46.473 --> 00:29:48.586
And LDOG TSUL means

00:29:48.586 --> 00:29:51.727
if you meditate on emptiness,

00:29:51.727 --> 00:29:54.640
if you get used to emptiness,

00:29:54.640 --> 00:29:59.640
you can clean out the crap
in your life, and guess what?

00:29:59.675 --> 00:30:04.575
If you, one by one, clean
out the parts of your life

00:30:05.504 --> 00:30:09.220
that aren't very nice,
or aren't very happy,

00:30:09.220 --> 00:30:11.215
or aren't very successful,

00:30:11.215 --> 00:30:14.180
there's this weird experience at the end.

00:30:14.180 --> 00:30:17.480
All you're left with is success.

00:30:17.480 --> 00:30:20.771
All you're left with is great happiness,

00:30:20.771 --> 00:30:23.320
great satisfaction, wonderful life.

00:30:23.320 --> 00:30:25.496
If you dig away,

00:30:25.496 --> 00:30:26.804
it's like cleaning out your house, right?

00:30:26.804 --> 00:30:29.167
Like nowadays I forgot why

00:30:29.167 --> 00:30:34.167
I'm trying to clean out my
house, and I go room by room,

00:30:34.187 --> 00:30:37.875
mostly tchotchkes,
mostly dumb little things

00:30:37.875 --> 00:30:39.135
I have in my house.

00:30:39.135 --> 00:30:42.557
I don't use them. I
don't think about them.

00:30:42.557 --> 00:30:45.213
I don't even look at them.

00:30:45.213 --> 00:30:48.181
And if you're just patient,

00:30:48.181 --> 00:30:51.632
and you're methodical,
and you work one by one

00:30:51.632 --> 00:30:54.020
through the garbage in your house

00:30:54.020 --> 00:30:57.598
and get rid of it mercilessly,

00:30:57.598 --> 00:30:59.403
like don't even compromise, don't say,

00:30:59.403 --> 00:31:02.986
"Maybe Rosa would like
