00:46:05.482 --> 00:46:08.981
Nagarjuna would find
this deeply disturbing.

00:46:08.981 --> 00:46:10.725
He would say, "Why?

00:46:10.725 --> 00:46:12.861
"It's the same equities.

00:46:12.861 --> 00:46:16.681
"Why should one person
make and investment fail,

00:46:16.681 --> 00:46:18.054
"and why should another person

00:46:18.054 --> 00:46:22.714
make a very similar
investment and succeed. Why?"

00:46:22.714 --> 00:46:24.673
It really bothered him.

00:46:24.673 --> 00:46:28.398
So, Nagarjuna's approach for us

00:46:29.464 --> 00:46:34.464
is to say, "Look, this
stuff, it bothers me.

00:46:34.570 --> 00:46:35.985
I don't get it."

00:46:35.985 --> 00:46:37.885
And that's how he teaches.

00:46:37.885 --> 00:46:41.794
He says, "I don't get it
why Tim should be there

00:46:41.794 --> 00:46:46.794
"his whole life, but his body's
changing every millisecond.

00:46:47.088 --> 00:46:49.293
"His mind is changing every millisecond.

00:46:49.293 --> 00:46:52.360
"Shouldn't Tim be a different
Tim in the second millisecond

00:46:52.360 --> 00:46:54.467
"if he doesn't have
the same body and mind?

00:46:54.467 --> 00:46:58.000
It bothers me. Something
here ain't right."

00:46:58.000 --> 00:46:59.432
Something here ain't right.

00:46:59.432 --> 00:47:01.803
If you're starting to feel like that,

00:47:01.803 --> 00:47:04.902
then you're starting to
understand Nagarjuna's approach.

00:47:04.902 --> 00:47:07.109
Things don't look right.

00:47:07.109 --> 00:47:10.526
If things are coming from their own side,

00:47:11.676 --> 00:47:14.215
thing's look fishy. Something's wrong.

00:47:14.215 --> 00:47:16.988
And his whole life was spent

00:47:16.988 --> 00:47:19.636
trying to figure out how to make it right.

00:47:19.636 --> 00:47:23.304
All right, let's see
you in the next class.

00:47:23.304 --> 00:47:25.637
And we'll get to the answer.

